  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe predominant feature in the synoptic pattern is a deep upper-\nlevel low stretched over the Great Lakes and Canadian Maritimes. A \nsteep ridge centered over the Rockies is therefore resulting in \nnortherly/northwesterly flow into the Gulf South to start out \ntoday. Shortwave troughs pivoting through the jet stream around the \nupper-level low will continue to reinforce cooler and drier air in \nour region with successive cold fronts into the weekend. The coldest \nair is likely to arrive by Friday night as a 1025+ mb surface high \nslips into the Central Plains.\n\nDepending on low-level flow and any cloud cover, a Light Freeze is \nlikely for at least parts of Mississippi north of I-20 and east of I-\n55 Friday night and/or Saturday night. Areas of frost are possible \nfarther south and west across most of the area either night with any \nlight winds and clear skies. A Freeze Warning is likely to be \nissued, though this weekend also marks the climatological average \nfirst freeze for may locations. Any headlines would likely be the \nlast for the season as drought has already brought the local growing \nseason to an end. Local hazard graphics have been updated to include \nan \"Elevated\" threat for freezing temperatures to highlight the \npotential for several hours below freezing both nights. Be sure \nfamily, neighbors, and pets have a warm place to stay if heating is \nnot available.\n\nBreezy and dry weather in the wake of this morning's cold front will \nresult in Limited fire danger conditions today, and expect drier \nconditions still tomorrow as winds persist. Local hazard graphic for \nfire danger was extended through Friday. Heed any local burn bans in \nplace, and avoid open flames or sparks near dry grass and \nvegetation.\n\nThe pattern looks to break down early next week, with a bit of \nsouthern jet stream energy carrying a closed low out of the Southern \nPlains and along the Gulf Coast Sunday into Monday. This will bring \nsome low-end chances for rain on the northern edge of the surface \nlow, but primarily act to disrupt the radiational cooling pattern \nfrom the weekend. Confidence in any widespread frost or freeze \nSunday night onward is low at this time. /NF/",
